These can be logical modules within one service initially. Separate agents or microservices are only necessary when they improve ownership, deployment, or isolation.
For human review, LangGraph supports persisted interrupts and resuming the same thread; production use requires a durable checkpointer. Add a worker/recovery mechanism to resume failed or waiting runs rather than treating checkpoint storage as the complete execution platform.[1][2]
2. Data and API contracts
Assessment input
Your minimum request can contain an address, class code, and effective date. Include a business name or identifier when available to reduce ambiguity.
{
  "mode": "FULL_ASSESSMENT",
  "business": {
    "name": "Example Fabrication LLC",
    "address": {
      "street": "123 Example Road",
      "city": "Bentonville",
      "state": "AR",
      "postal_code": "72712",
      "country": "US"
    },
    "business_id": null
  },
  "classification": {
    "class_code": "PROVIDER_SPECIFIC_CODE",
    "code_system": "CARRIER_CLASS",
    "source": "UNDERWRITER"
  },
  "effective_date": "2026-11-01",
  "line_of_business": "GENERAL_LIABILITY",
  "question": null
}


Important design choices:
Keep class codes as strings, preserving leading zeros.
Require a code-system identifier; do not silently treat a carrier code, NAICS code, and another classification system as interchangeable.
Treat supplied classification as an input assertion and compare it with discovered operations.
Require effective_date for appetite analysis rather than silently using today.
Include product, jurisdiction, and carrier/program identifiers if the appetite API uses them.
Obtain tenant identity and user permissions from authentication—not model-generated arguments.
