Deterministically calculated metric.
Model inference.
Hypothetical assumption.
Historical analysis also needs a temporal distinction: the proposed policy effective date, the loss valuation date, and the date information became known are not the same. An appetite rule valid on an effective date does not by itself make the entire assessment a historical reconstruction.
3. Smart routing and execution
Use a hybrid router: the LLM interprets the question, while deterministic policies validate and execute the plan.
Routing examples
Request
Required tools
Calls deliberately omitted
“Assess this business.”
Discovery → app and losses after prerequisites are met
Optional enrichment unless a material evidence gap warrants it
“Is this class in appetite on November 1?”
Appetite; discovery only if identity/classification needs resolution
Loss history
“What concerns appear in its loss history?”
Loss history; discovery if no confirmed entity ID exists
Appetite unless eligibility is also requested
“Does this business actually perform welding?”
Business discovery
Appetite and losses
“Why did you recommend referral?”
Read the assessment and supporting evidence
New external calls unless evidence needs refreshing
“What if the business stops welding?”
Scenario branch using existing evidence; appetite lookup if approved reclassification is required
Changes to the actual business record


Do not make every question trigger the entire assessment.
Execution sequence
Validate the request and determine whether it is a full assessment, focused question, explanation, or hypothetical scenario.
Resolve the business identity when required.
Check the supplied class code against discovered operations; request review for material discrepancies.
Build a dependency plan with required and optional tools.
Execute independent calls concurrently when their prerequisites are satisfied.
Normalize results into evidence records.
Calculate approved loss metrics outside the LLM.
Identify unresolved contradictions and missing evidence.
Generate the risk analysis from the evidence bundle.
Validate and either publish the response or pause for review.
An appetite lookup may run alongside discovery when its inputs are already authoritative. However, do not publish a business-specific conclusion until identity and classification consistency checks pass.
Router output
{
  "intent": "LOSS_RISK_QUESTION",
  "required_facts": [
    "confirmed_business_identity",
    "loss_history",
    "loss_history_coverage"
  ],
  "plan": [
    {
      "tool": "discover_business",
      "condition": "confirmed_business_id_missing"
    },
    {
      "tool": "get_loss_history",
      "depends_on": ["confirmed_business_identity"]
    }
  ],
  "omitted_tools": [
    {
      "tool": "get_appetite",
      "reason": "Question does not request appetite or eligibility"
    }
  ],
  "output_scope": "LOSS_HISTORY_ONLY"
}


This is a proposed plan, not permission to execute. The policy router must enforce the registered tool schema, user access, data scope, dependencies, and call limits.
Guardrails
Use an allowlisted tool registry; never allow arbitrary URLs or model-written API requests.
Treat provider content as evidence, not instructions to the agent.
Retry transient failures within a bounded budget; do not retry authorization failures indefinitely.
Cache only within approved tenant boundaries, source-use terms, and freshness windows.
Record cache use and original retrieval time.
Replan only when new evidence introduces a material gap.
Stop when required evidence is complete, blocked, unavailable, or the budget is exhausted.
Require review for ambiguous identity, conflicting classifications, incomplete critical evidence, or unauthorized decision requests.
Define completeness by intent. A focused appetite question can be complete without losses; a full assessment may not be.
4. Analysis and underwriter experience
Keep three outputs separate
Output
Source
Example
Appetite
Authoritative appetite API
Acceptable
Risk assessment
Evidence synthesis and approved calculations
Recurring fire-related losses; mitigation evidence missing
Disposition recommendation
Carrier-approved decision policy
REFER


A business can be Preferred by appetite and still require referral because of its individual loss experience. Conversely, favorable risk characteristics must not cause the model to change an Undesirable appetite result.
Define how Undesirable maps to workflow behavior in a governed carrier policy; do not assume it always means automatic decline.
Loss analysis
Normalize financial fields before calculating metrics:
Paid indemnity and paid expense.
Outstanding indemnity and expense reserves.
Recoveries, where supplied.
Provider-reported incurred amount.
Loss date, report date, claim status, and valuation date.
Coverage line and location/entity scope.
Use provider definitions to avoid adding reserves twice to an already-incurred total. Report frequency and severity with the observed period and scope. Do not calculate a loss ratio without the appropriate earned-premium denominator.
The LLM should explain calculated patterns—not perform the underlying financial aggregation.
