Governance and testing
The NAIC AI Model Bulletin emphasizes governance, risk management, documentation, and controls for risks including inaccurate outcomes, unfair discrimination, and lack of explainability. Use it as a governance reference, while validating applicable jurisdiction-specific requirements with compliance.[5][6]
Prioritize tests for:
Multiple businesses at one address.
Operations inconsistent with the supplied class code.
Appetite validity-date boundaries.
Loss-history timeout versus verified zero losses.
Claims belonging to another entity or location.
Duplicate claims and changing valuation dates.
Unsupported findings or fabricated evidence references.
Prompt injection embedded in discovery responses.
Cross-tenant access attempts.
Standalone questions calling unnecessary tools.
Restart and resume without duplicate external writes.
Measure tool-selection accuracy, evidence completeness, unsupported-claim rate, identity-resolution review rate, underwriter edits, latency, cost per assessment, and recovery success.
Implementation sequence
Build typed API adapters, normalized evidence schemas, and an auditable deterministic assessment workflow.
Add question interpretation and policy-controlled tool routing.
Add source-grounded risk narratives, output validation, and human review.
Add caching, targeted replanning, recovery testing, and production evaluations.
Expand optional enrichment only where it measurably improves risk assessment.
The implementation principle is simple: give the agent flexibility in deciding what evidence to retrieve, but keep identity, appetite categories, calculations, permissions, and final underwriting authority under explicit control.
